\section{Materials and methods}

\subsection{Study design and platform overview}
This study was designed to develop and perform a workflow validation of DentalClaw, a natural-language-driven platform for lowering the technical barrier of dental imaging artificial intelligence (AI) research, built on OpenClaw \cite{openclaw}. The aim was not to introduce a new task-specific segmentation backbone, but to determine whether a clinician-facing platform could reduce the technical burden of dental AI experimentation by converting natural-language research requests into executable and reviewable workflows. In this context, DentalClaw was designed to address a common practical gap in dental AI research: clinicians and dental researchers may understand the research question and desired task, but often lack the computational expertise required to prepare datasets, configure experiments, launch training pipelines, and organize outputs reproducibly.

DentalClaw adopts a workflow-oriented architecture comprising a natural-language task orchestration layer, a reusable skill layer, and shared platform services for case representation, experiment tracking, audit logging, and human review. Instead of requiring users to assemble scripts manually for each experiment, the platform interprets task instructions expressed in natural language and translates them into standardized workflow steps, including data ingestion, dataset preparation, preprocessing, model execution, evaluation, and structured reporting. Major workflow events, configuration choices, validation outcomes, and generated outputs are stored in machine-readable form to support repeatability and retrospective review.

