(e.g., ``segment teeth with TTA and ensemble'') and were resolved during intent
parsing with automatic dataset defaulting to TDD when unspecified.
}

\DIFaddend \subsection{Workflow execution and automated dataset preparation}

All input datasets were transformed into a canonical case-level representation to support consistent downstream processing across modalities. This standardization step was particularly important in a natural-language-driven research platform, because users were not required to manually configure low-level preprocessing logic or dataset checks for each experiment. Each case record contained references to image data, annotation files when available, task labels, modality information, metadata fields relevant to acquisition and preprocessing, and workflow status indicators. This representation enabled 2D panoramic radiographs and 3D CBCT volumes to be managed under a common framework while preserving modality-specific attributes required for task execution.

Before model development, DentalClaw applied structured dataset checking and preparation procedures as built-in workflow safeguards intended to make user-initiated experiments more reliable and reviewable. These procedures included completeness checks for missing images, absent annotations, malformed label files, and file-level corruption; image--annotation consistency checks; annotation integrity checks for empty masks, duplicated labels, and invalid label values; label normalization for dental numbering or class schema; plausibility and topology-aware checks; metadata parsing for acquisition-related descriptors such as voxel spacing and dimensionality; and split verification for duplicated identifiers, repeated cases, or other indicators of potential train--test leakage. For each flagged issue, the platform recorded the issue category, affected case, and downstream handling decision, including automatic normalization, exclusion from downstream use, or flagging for manual review.

DentalClaw then generated a task-specific execution plan including preprocessing rules, training configuration, split assignment, output directories, and evaluation settings. The appropriate workflow skills for preprocessing, model training, inference, metric computation, and report rendering were subsequently invoked in a reproducible order. Intermediate outputs were stored together with execution logs and workflow summaries.

\subsection{Validation workflows and outcome measures}

The validation strategy comprised two complementary components: (1) successful translation of representative natural-language research requests into executable 2D and 3D dental imaging workflows; and (2) structured output generation for workflow review and result traceability.

Two representative task workflows were executed through the same platform logic. In the panoramic workflow, panoramic images and annotations were imported, checked, assigned to a 2D task-specific configuration, and processed through automated preprocessing, training, inference, and metric computation. In the CBCT workflow, volumetric image data, annotations, and metadata were ingested and checked before task-specific preprocessing, model execution, inference, and aggregation of case-level results. In both workflows, structured summaries of preparation findings, workflow status, and predictions were generated automatically. The 3D visualizations and surface renderings of the segmentation masks were generated using 3D Slicer (version 5.10.0) \cite{Fedorov2012}. The aim of these workflows was not only to obtain segmentation performance estimates, but also to assess whether DentalClaw could convert research intent into executable workflows and generate review-oriented outputs suitable for inspection by dentists and clinical researchers.

Evaluation was performed at two levels. First, workflow execution endpoints included successful interpretation of representative natural-language task requests, successful completion of major workflow stages, number of imported and retained cases, dominant preparation findings, runtime of major workflow stages, and successful generation of structured outputs. Second, task-level performance for the two representative segmentation workflows was summarized using standard overlap- and boundary-based metrics, including Dice \cite{Dice1945} similarity coefficient, and distance-based measures where appropriate. These task-level results were interpreted as supportive indicators of end-to-end workflow feasibility rather than as the primary basis for claiming state-of-the-art segmentation performance.

\subsection{Skill-based configuration case studies}

To further evaluate whether DentalClaw could encode and reuse practical model-configuration knowledge within traceable workflows, we conducted two skill-based configuration case studies covering both 2D panoramic radiograph segmentation and 3D CBCT segmentation. These experiments were not designed to establish new state-of-the-art segmentation models. Instead, they examined whether common but practically important configuration strategies could be formalized as reusable DentalClaw skills and executed under the same audit, logging, and reporting framework.

The first case study focused on the TDD-derived 32-class panoramic tooth segmentation task. In this 2D workflow, DentalClaw represented model-tuning strategies as reusable skills, including intensity augmentation, optimizer selection, learning-rate scheduling, and test-time augmentation. All configuration trials used the same audited dataset, predefined train/validation/test split, and evaluation pipeline. DentalClaw recorded the selected configuration, execution logs, validation metrics, test metrics, prediction outputs, and generated reports for each trial. The best-performing configuration was selected based on validation performance and then evaluated on the held-out test set.

The second case study focused on 3D CBCT segmentation. In this workflow, DentalClaw represented multi-model fusion as a reusable skill rather than as an ad hoc post-processing step. The fusion skill coordinated the outputs of multiple segmentation models under a predefined inference and fusion protocol, and recorded the participating models, fusion settings, intermediate predictions, final segmentation outputs, evaluation metrics, and structured reports. This case study was used to assess whether DentalClaw could support reusable skill execution not only for parameter-level model tuning in 2D images, but also for model-level orchestration and fusion in 3D volumetric segmentation.

\subsection{Statistical analysis}

Results were summarized descriptively. Workflow-level outcomes included input and eligible cases, completion status, preparation findings, generated outputs, and stage runtime. Segmentation performance was reported using the metrics generated by the evaluation pipeline. No inferential statistical testing was performed because this study focused on workflow feasibility, automation efficiency, and output completeness.

