DentalClaw was validated on two representative public datasets covering two methodologically distinct 
dental imaging scenarios: the TDD dataset for 2D panoramic radiograph segmentation and the ToothFairy dataset for 3D CBCT segmentation. In both settings, the platform completed the full workflow chain from dataset ingestion and automated dataset preparation to preprocessing, model training, inference, evaluation, and structured output generation. Despite substantial differences in data organization, preprocessing requirements, and model execution logic between the two tasks, DentalClaw preserved a unified workflow framework while adapting automatically to modality-specific characteristics of panoramic radiographs and volumetric CBCT scans.

A key advantage of DentalClaw was that these workflows could be executed with minimal manual coordination. Rather than relying on fragmented scripts and repeated user intervention at each stage, the platform standardized dataset handling, preparation logic, model execution, and result aggregation within a single orchestrated pipeline. This reduced the dependence on manually assembled procedures and enabled the user to obtain execution-ready results through a more consistent and reproducible process.

In addition to completing the computational pipeline, DentalClaw generated structured user-facing outputs after workflow execution, summarizing task configuration, dataset status, training records, evaluation results, and review-relevant findings in a unified format. A representative example is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:complete_training}. Instead of returning isolated model files, intermediate folders, or console logs alone, the platform produced a reviewable workflow artifact that consolidated the key information needed for result interpretation and downstream checking. Quantitative workflow-level results, including imported cases, retained cases, preparation outcomes, and generated outputs, are summarized in Table~\ref{tab:workflow_validation}.


