\DIFaddend \subsection{Private object detection workflow and lesion-focused refinement}

To further evaluate DentalClaw on private clinical data, we conducted an object detection workflow using our own dataset of 290 dental radiographs. The dataset was annotated for four target categories: pulp, root canal fillings, fillings, and periapical lesion. DentalClaw standardized the annotation format, checked image--annotation consistency, generated a fixed train--validation--test split, and executed the detection workflow with traceable training and evaluation records. The split contained 203 training images, 58 validation images, and 29 test images. For periapical lesions, the corresponding numbers of lesion instances were 66, 19, and 10, respectively.

In the initial four-class detection experiment, DentalClaw trained and evaluated a detector on the validation set. As shown in Table~\ref{tab:private_detection_four_class}, the overall mAP$_{50}$ was 0.613 and the mAP$_{50-95}$ was 0.335. The detector achieved relatively higher performance for pulp, root canal fillings, and fillings. In contrast, periapical lesion detection showed substantially lower performance, with a precision of 0.208, recall of 0.071, mAP$_{50}$ of 0.076, and mAP$_{50-95}$ of 0.025. This result indicated that the lesion category was the main bottleneck in the private detection workflow.

